@node C33 Opcodes
@section Opcodes

@cindex C33 opcodes
@cindex opcodes for C33
@code{@value{AS}} implements the instructions of the selected core.

@cindex @code{ext} instruction, C33
An @code{ext} instruction supplies 13 more bits of the immediate or
displacement of the instruction that follows it, and one or two
@code{ext} can precede an instruction.  @code{@value{AS}} also provides
instructions that insert the @code{ext} prefixes themselves, named by a
letter before the instruction's own name:

@table @code
@item x
@samp{xld.w}, @samp{xadd}, @samp{xcall}, @samp{xjp}, @samp{xjrne},
@samp{xbset} and the other @samp{x}-prefixed instructions take a 32-bit
immediate, a displacement, or a symbol, and use as many @code{ext}
prefixes as the value needs, two for a symbol.  @samp{xcall}, @samp{xjp}
and the conditional @samp{xjr@var{cc}} reach the whole address space.

@item s
@samp{scall}, @samp{sjp} and @samp{sjr@var{cc}} use one @code{ext} prefix
and reach 2 megabytes in either direction.

@item a
@samp{ald.b}, @samp{ald.h}, @samp{ald.ub}, @samp{ald.uh} and
@samp{ald.w} exist only for the Advanced core.  Without
@option{-medda32} they reach @samp{[@var{symbol}+@var{offset}]} from
@code{%dp} with one @code{ext} prefix.
@end table

@cindex relocation operators, C33
@cindex C33 relocation operators
The following operators select part of an address for an @code{ext}
prefix or for the instruction it extends.  Each also has an upper-case
spelling.

@table @code
@item @var{symbol}@@h
@itemx @var{symbol}@@m
@itemx @var{symbol}@@l
Bits 31 to 19, 18 to 6 and 5 to 0 of the address of @var{symbol}.  For
example, this loads the address of @code{sym} into @code{%r4}:

@smallexample
ext     sym@@h
ext     sym@@m
ld.w    %r4,sym@@l
@end smallexample

@item @var{symbol}@@ah
@itemx @var{symbol}@@al
Bits 25 to 13 and 12 to 0 of the address of @var{symbol}.

@item @var{symbol}@@rh
@itemx @var{symbol}@@rm
@itemx @var{symbol}@@rl
The high, middle and low parts of the PC-relative displacement to
@var{symbol}, for two @code{ext} prefixes and a branch or call.

@item doff_hi(@var{symbol})
@itemx doff_lo(@var{symbol})
Bits 25 to 13 and 12 to 0 of the offset of @var{symbol} from
@code{__dp}, the default data pointer that @code{%r15} holds:

@smallexample
ext     doff_hi(var)
ext     doff_lo(var)
ld.w    %r4,[%r15]
@end smallexample

@item dpoff_h(@var{symbol})
@itemx dpoff_m(@var{symbol})
@itemx dpoff_l(@var{symbol})
The offset of @var{symbol} from @code{__dp} in three parts, for access
through @code{%dp} on the Advanced core.

@item goff_lo(@var{symbol})
@itemx soff_hi(@var{symbol})
@itemx soff_lo(@var{symbol})
@itemx toff_hi(@var{symbol})
@itemx toff_lo(@var{symbol})
@itemx zoff_hi(@var{symbol})
@itemx zoff_lo(@var{symbol})
The offset of @var{symbol} from the base of the G, S, T or Z data area,
the symbols @code{__gdp}, @code{__sdp}, @code{__tdp} and @code{__zdp};
@samp{hi} is bits 25 to 13 and @samp{lo} bits 12 to 0.
@end table

The following operators are accepted in data directives such as
@code{.long}:

@table @code
@item funcdesc(@var{symbol})
The address of the canonical FDPIC function descriptor of
@var{symbol}.

@item tlsmod(@var{symbol})
The ID of the module that defines the thread-local variable
@var{symbol}, filled in by the dynamic linker.

@item tlsoff(@var{symbol})
The offset of @var{symbol} in its module's thread-local storage.

@item tlsie(@var{symbol})
The offset of @var{symbol} from the thread pointer, filled in by the
linker or the dynamic linker (the initial-exec model).

@item tlsle(@var{symbol})
The offset of @var{symbol} from the thread pointer, fixed at link time
(the local-exec model).
@end table
